sec.p:
An orientation selective index (OSI) was defined as follows:
OSI=|∑kR(θk)e−2iθk|∑kR(θk) \begin{document}$$\displaystyle O S I=\frac{\left|\sum_{k} R\left(\theta_{k}\right) e^{-2 i \theta_{k}}\right|}{\sum_{k} R\left(\theta_{k}\right)}$$\end{document}
which is equivalent to 1 − circular variance in circular statistics. Note that the factor of 2 in the OSI equation makes angles separated by 180° equivalent, so the measure quantifies orientation rather than direction.

A direction selective index (DSI) was defined as follows:


DSI=|∑kR(θk)e−iθk|∑kR(θk)
\begin{document}$$\displaystyle D S I=\frac{\left|\sum_{k} R\left(\theta_{k}\right) e^{-i \theta_{k}}\right|}{\sum_{k} R\left(\theta_{k}\right)}$$\end{document}



The preferred orientation was calculated as follows:


θpref=12arctan⁡(∑kR(θk)sin⁡(2θk)∑kR(θk)cos⁡(2θk))
\begin{document}$$\displaystyle \theta_{\text {pref }}=\frac{1}{2} \arctan \left(\frac{\sum_{k} R\left(\theta_{k}\right) \sin \left(2 \theta_{k}\right)}{\sum_{k} R\left(\theta_{k}\right) \cos \left(2 \theta_{k}\right)}\right)$$\end{document}



The circular Pearson correlation (rcirc) between θp𝑕y and θpref in orientation space was defined as follows:


rcirc=∑j(sin⁡(2θphy,j)−<sin⁡(2θphy)>)(sin⁡(2θori,j)−<sin⁡(2θori)>)∑j(sin⁡(2θphy,j)−<sin⁡(2θphy)>)∑k(sin⁡(2θori,k)−<sin⁡(2θori)>)
\begin{document}$$\displaystyle r_{\text {circ }}=\frac{\sum_{j}\left(\sin \left(2 \theta_{\text {phy }, j}\right)-<\sin \left(2 \theta_{\text {phy }}\right)>\right)\left(\sin \left(2 \theta_{\text {ori }, j}\right)-<\sin \left(2 \theta_{\text {ori }}\right)>\right)}{\sum_{j}\left(\sin \left(2 \theta_{\text {phy }, j}\right)-<\sin \left(2 \theta_{\text {phy }}\right)>\right) \sum_{k}\left(\sin \left(2 \theta_{\text {ori }, k}\right)-<\sin \left(2 \theta_{\text {ori }}\right)>\right)}$$\end{document}



To demix spatially overlapping dendrites and visualize spatial components of apparent dendritic structures that were otherwise indistinguishable in the correlation image, we utilized non-negative matrix decomposition to decompose 3d video Y(t) into products of spatial footprint Ai and corresponding temporal fluorescence traces Ci and background noise B(t):


Y(t)=∑iAiCi(t)+B(t),s.t.Aj≥0,andCi≥0
\begin{document}$$\displaystyle Y(t)= \sum_iA_iC_i(t)  + B(t), \quad  s.t.A_j \geq  0, \quad and \quad C_{i} \geq 0$$\end{document}



using the alternative least square algorithm (Berry et al., 2007).

To analyze the orientation bias of a BK WAC dendritic tree, we modified the OSI analysis, described above for fluorescence measurements and previously to characterize cortical neuron responses (Liang et al., 2023; Niell & Stryker, 2008). We considered every dendrite to have an R value of 1, such that the morphological OSI (mOSI) equation simplifies to the following:
mOSI=|∑kei2θk|N \begin{document}$$\displaystyle m O S I=\frac{\left|\sum_{k} e^{i 2 \theta_{k}}\right|}{\mathrm{N}}$$\end{document}
where N is the number of dendrites. To evaluate the mOSI value relative to the null hypothesis, we generated Monte Carlo null distributions of mOSI values for each relevant number of dendrites observed in the BK WAC sample (4-13 dendrites). For each number of dendrites (dn), we generated 10,000 samples with dendrite angles drawn independently from a uniform distribution over 0-360°. For each cell, we calculated a one-sided p-value as the fraction of simulated mOSI values greater than or equal to the observed mOSI, using the null distribution with the same number of dendrites. Under the null hypothesis, the p-value distributions would follow a uniform distribution, and we evaluated the sample p-value distribution, either for all BK WACs or from the ON BK WACs alone, relative to the uniform distribution using a Kolmogorov-Smirnov test (one-sample test).
